          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 6063,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 826429025,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Agreed. My knowledge in that regard is based on various burns I got in the past about those issues. And I also assume, that the warning on the Qt docs is mostly derived from the fact, that they are also available on various embedded targets, and especially the risc ones have some weird issues (although I agree, int probably will not be affected that much).\n\nBut again, makes sense to make this code as predefined settings based as possible.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2498449,
            "username": "rknall",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Roland Knall",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9e1d973c5007651b2ca47071f80324c2de5349d873e365cf0761df5542dfe382?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/rknall"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-01-31T10:30:54.455Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-01-31T10:30:54.455Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 137717612,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": true,
          "resolved_by": {
            "id": 2498449,
            "username": "rknall",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Roland Knall",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9e1d973c5007651b2ca47071f80324c2de5349d873e365cf0761df5542dfe382?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/rknall"
          },
          "resolved_at": "2022-02-01T21:24:16.749Z",
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 6063,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 826499567,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> and especially the risc ones have some weird issues\n\nRISC *embedded targets* may have some weird issues, but the sort of issues we'd be most likely to run into on RISC-based *general purpose* systems are\n\n- lack of support for unaligned accesses (if we're dereferencing unaligned pointers, we should stop doing so);\n- memory ordering other than strong memory ordering;\n- byte order on older RISCs (the CPUs on which most people are doing development are probably x86, and thus little-endian, and older RISCs tend to run big-endian, although newer ones tend to run little-endian; then again, z/Architecture doesn't have a choice, it always runs big-endian).\n\nFor whatever reason, Apple doesn't mention the first of those in [\"Addressing Architectural Differences in Your macOS Code\"](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/apple-silicon/addressing-architectural-differences-in-your-macos-code); perhaps the M series CPU cores support it, so as not to rudely surprise developers used to it working on x86.\n\nThey *do* mention the second in the \"Synchronize Access to Shared Data in Memory\" subsection of the aforementioned document.\n\n(They don't mention the third because their ARM CPUs run little-endian, just like x86.)\n\n> int probably will not be affected that much\n\nInteger data sizes and pointer sizes are not the sort of issues we'd be likely to run into on RISC-based general purpose systems.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
